  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "num": "01",
      "en": "The {notion} that we live in someone else's video game, akin to a highly advanced {VR} experience, is {irresistible} to many.In science-fiction {movies} , we often see worlds where characters live inside a {simulation} controlled by a {master} programmer and {suspect} nothing.Searching the term “simulation hypothesis”( (模拟假说) ) returns {numerous} results that debate whether the {universe} is a computer simulation.This idea has gained attention with the rapid advancement of technology, including the development of {robots} and virtual environments that can mimic real-world physics, such as {gravity} and {orbit} .Unfortunately, we will probably never know whether it's true.We can, instead, use this idea to advance scientific knowledge.",
      "cn": "我们生活在别人的电子游戏中，宛如一场极其先进的 <u> VR </u> 体验——这一 <u> 想法 </u> 对许多人而言有着 <u> 无法抵制的 </u> 吸引力。在科幻 <u> 电影 </u> 中，我们常常会看到这样的世界：角色们生活在一个由 <u> 顶级 </u> 程序员控制的 <u> 模拟 </u> 世界中，却对此毫无 <u> 察觉 </u> 。搜索“模拟假说”一词，会得到 <u> 无数 </u> 探讨 <u> 宇宙 </u> 是否是计算机模拟产物的结果。随着科技的飞速发展，这一想法受到了广泛关注，其中包括能够模拟诸如 <u> 重力 </u> 和（天体运行） <u> 轨道 </u> 等现实世界物理规律的 <u> 机器人 </u> 和虚拟环境的开发。遗憾的是，我们或许永远无法知晓这一假说是否正确。不过，我们可以借助这一想法来推动科学知识的进步。"
    },
    {
      "num": "02",
      "en": "The 18th-century philosopher Kant argued that the universe ultimately consists of things-in-themselves that are {unknowable} .According to him, although objective reality exists, we cannot {deny} that our minds play a necessary role in structuring and shaping our perceptions. {Modern} science has {revealed} that our {perceptual} experience of the world is the result of many stages of processing by {sensory} systems and cognitive ( (认知的) ) functions in the brain.Scientists can study {inputs} and {outputs} , but cannot fully {expose} the processes of mental operations. {Nobody} knows exactly what happens within this {black} box.Some scholars {insist} that advances in science will effectively open the black box.Others {oppose} this view: if empirical ( (实证的) ) experience fails to reveal reality, reasoning won't reveal reality either since it relies on concepts and words that are {contingent} on our social, cultural and psychological histories.Again, a black box.",
      "cn": "18世纪哲学家康德认为，宇宙归根结底由 <u> 不可知的 </u> 自在之物组成。按照他的说法，尽管客观现实是存在的，但我们不能 <u> 否认 </u> ，我们的意识在构建和塑造认知方面发挥着不可或缺的作用。 <u> 现代 </u> 科学 <u> 揭示 </u> ，我们对世界的 <u> 感知 </u> 体验是 <u> 感官 </u> 系统和大脑认知功能经过多个处理阶段的结果。科学家可以研究 <u> 输入 </u> 和 <u> 输出 </u> 的信息，但无法完全 <u> 揭示 </u> 心理运作的过程。 <u> 没有人 </u> 知道这个“ <u> 黑 </u> 匣子”里到底发生了什么。一些学者 <u> 坚称 </u> ，科学的进步将有效地为我们开启这个黑匣子。另一些人则 <u> 反对 </u> 这种观点，认为如果实证经验无法揭示现实，那么推理也无法揭示，因为推理依赖概念和词语，而这些概念和词语又 <u> 取决于 </u> 我们的社会、文化和心理历程。同样，这又是一个“黑匣子”问题。"
    },
    {
      "num": "03",
      "en": "So, if we accept that the universe is unknowable, we also accept we will never know if we live in a computer simulation.And then, we can shift our {inquiry} from “Is the universe a computer simulation?”to “Can we model the universe as a computer simulation?”Modeling reality is what we do.This practice is more significant than ever: modeling the behavior of {hydrogen} {atoms} has led to new {inventions} in {nuclear} energy and technology; models predict natural disasters like {hurricanes} and {landslides} , helping in planning {rescue} operations; when {launching} {rockets} and placing {satellites} , models of space {mechanics} have been playing an essential part.",
      "cn": "因此，如果我们承认宇宙是不可知的，那我们也得承认，我们永远无法知道自己是否生活在计算机模拟的世界中。由此，我们就可以把我们的 <u> 探究问题 </u> 从“宇宙是计算机模拟出来的吗？”转变为“我们能否用计算机构建出宇宙模型？”对现实进行建模正是我们一直在做的事。这种实践比以往任何时候都更为重要—— <u> 氢原子 </u> 行为的建模促成了 <u> 核 </u> 能及技术方面的新 <u> 发明 </u> ；模型能够预测 <u> 飓风 </u> 和 <u> 山体滑坡 </u> 等自然灾害，帮助规划 <u> 救援 </u> 行动；在 <u> 发射火箭 </u> 和部署 <u> 卫星 </u> 时，空间 <u> 力学 </u> 模型一直发挥着至关重要的作用。"
    },
    {
      "num": "04",
      "en": "To {facilitate} our comprehension of the world, we build models based on {conceptual} metaphors that are familiar to us.In Newton's {era} , we imagined the universe as a clock.In Einstein's, we uncovered the standard model of particle ( (粒子) ) physics.",
      "cn": "为了 <u> 便于 </u> 理解世界，我们基于熟悉的隐喻 <u> 概念 </u> 建立模型。在牛顿 <u> 时代 </u> ，我们把宇宙想象成一座时钟。在爱因斯坦时代，我们发现了粒子物理学的标准模型。"
    },
    {
      "num": "05",
      "en": "Now that we are in the information age, we have new concepts such as the computer, information processing, {virtual} reality, and simulation. {Unsurprisingly} , these new concepts inspire us to build new models of the universe.Models are not the reality, however.There is no point in arguing if the universe is a clock, a set of particles or an output of {computation} .All these models are tools to deal with the {unknown} and to make {discoveries} .And the more tools we have, the more effective and {insightful} we can become.",
      "cn": "如今，我们身处信息时代，拥有了诸如计算机、信息处理、 <u> 虚拟 </u> 现实和模拟等新概念。 <u> 不出所料 </u> ，这些新概念激发我们建立关于宇宙的新模型。然而，模型并非现实。争论宇宙是一座时钟、一组粒子还是 <u> 计算 </u> 的输出结果并无意义。所有这些模型都是我们应对 <u> 未知 </u> 、探索 <u> 发现 </u> 的工具。我们拥有的工具越多，就会变得越高效、越 <u> 有洞察力 </u> 。 5NoOncib/NjiX02BpMIzP4m+378Hw1xTRxUdPaP/PFjKUFq4K2G7tp6ZLYoCE601yW1W39VMdLmovFdSHs4nVQ=="
    }
  ],
